- How to use `az` command map:
  - Issue lifecycle:
  - `az issue list --limit 20`, `az issue list --status in_progress --limit 20`, `az issue get <issue-id>`, `az issue create "Child task" [--json]` (immediate child work; auto-parents under `AZEDARACH_ISSUE_ID`), `az issue create --deferred "Later backlog task" [--json]` (standalone later work; does not auto-parent), `az issue update <issue-id> --status in_progress|blocked|open [--json]`, `az issue close <issue-id> [--json]`, `az issue finalize <issue-id> [--remove-worktree] [--json]`
  - `az issue update`: use `--update-impl` only when changing implementation assignments; status/title/notes updates do not need it.
  - `az issue bulk-create --input issues.json --json`, `az issue bulk-update --input updates.json --json`, `az issue update <issue-id> --design "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --notes "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --append-notes "..."`
  - Issue dependency graph:
  - `az issue dep add <issue-id> <depends-on-id> [--type blocks|related|parent-child|discovered-from] [--json]`, `az issue dep remove <issue-id> <depends-on-id> [--type blocks|related|parent-child|discovered-from] [--json]`
  - Orchestration:
  {{ if .OrchestrationViaNative }}- `orchestration.via` is `native`: use your harness-native subagent delegation commands/keywords to split and run child issues (for example "delegate", "spawn", or "subagent"). Keep using `az issue` for tracking state and dependencies.
  - `az orchestrate` and `az issue fanout` commands are optional in this mode; prefer native delegation flow unless you explicitly need CLI-managed readiness/session starts.
  {{ else if .OrchestrationViaAz }}
  - `az orchestrate status --root <issue-id> [--since <seq>] [--limit <n>] [--json]`
  - `az orchestrate watch --root <issue-id> --since <seq> [--jsonl] [--once]`
  - `az orchestrate start --root <issue-id> [--limit <n>] [--issue <issue-id> ...] [--json]`
  - `az orchestrate complete-check --root <issue-id> [--json]`
  - `orchestration.via` is `az`: use `az orchestrate` as the worker launch and coordination authority. Do not use harness-native subagent/delegation tooling for this graph unless the user explicitly overrides this or changes `orchestration.via` to `native`.
  - Use `az orchestrate status` for readiness snapshots, `watch` for observe-only mailbox/runnable updates, `start` to launch runnable leaf workers, and `complete-check` before closing the root issue.
  - Az orchestration loop:
    1. Shape the graph with child issues and dependency edges until each leaf is one-session-sized and independently mergeable.
    2. Run `az orchestrate status --root <issue-id> --json` to inspect runnable leaves, blocked leaves, active sessions, and the next mailbox sequence.
    3. Start only runnable leaves with `az orchestrate start --root <issue-id> --limit <n>`; use `--issue <issue-id>` for hand-picked starts when ordering matters.
    4. Watch progress with `az orchestrate watch --root <issue-id> --since <seq> --jsonl` or poll `status`; handle `worker-blocked`, dependency-ready, failed sessions, and newly runnable leaves before starting the next wave.
    5. Repeat status -> start -> watch until `az orchestrate complete-check --root <issue-id>` passes, then run final validation and close the root.
  - Small graph: 1-3 leaves, usually run `status`, then `start --limit 3`, then `complete-check` after workers finish.
  - Medium graph: 4-10 leaves, run bounded waves (`--limit 2` to `--limit 4`), watch mailbox events between waves, and start newly unblocked leaves only after dependency-ready/complete signals.
  - Large graph: 10+ leaves or multiple dependency phases, keep orchestration centralized; start with a low limit, use `status --json` as the source of truth for readiness, avoid launching sub-orchestrators, and do not close until complete-check and root validation both pass.
